name Ashes Of The Unreturned Sky
msg
*Author unknown; origin disputed*

When Scorn fell, the West believed the dragons were defeated. This belief persists because it is useful, not because it is true. Dragons did not vanish from fear or loss alone, but from judgment. The city built atop their dead was allowed to stand so that its people would never forget what they rested upon.

The texts record that dragons measure time by scars upon the world. Old Scorn is named a Scar-City, a place where blood, stone, and command were bound together. The Golden Lance of Lythander is not described as a weapon of slaying, but of binding?an instrument that fixed the sky?s pathways and forced dragonkind into exile without erasing them. Such acts create debt.

It is written that dragon teeth left embedded in the ruins were not abandoned trophies. They were markers. Each tooth names a fallen lineage, a broken pact, or a silenced voice. To remove them is to reopen an account thought dormant. The author warns that shaping these remains into tools or blades transforms memory into insult.

The dragons are said to have sworn no oath of peace. They chose waiting over ruin, believing mortals would one day disturb what they buried. When that moment comes, fire will not fall in rage, but in answer. The text emphasizes that vengeance delayed is not forgiveness denied?it is preparation.

Sstone remembers pressure, that bone remembers violence, the sky remembers being commanded. When Scorn?s foundations are mined deeply enough, the dragons will return?not to conquer the West, but to finish what was never concluded.
endmsg
