name On the Silent Brood: A Study of Draconic Egg Death
msg
On the Silent Brood: A Study of Draconic Egg Death

The death of a dragon egg is not a singular event, but a failure across many thresholds. In living clutches, an egg is not merely a vessel but an extension of the mother?s will?attuned to her breath, her heat, and her presence. When a brood dies intact, untouched by scavenger or flame, it suggests not accident but interruption.

This volume catalogs known instances of fossilized dragon egg remains, with particular attention paid to those found near the skeletal remains of a guarding mother. In each case, the mother dragon?s posture at death is consistent: coiled inward, wings partially mantled, skull angled toward the clutch. This is not the posture of retreat or exhaustion. It is the posture of refusal.

The fossilization of eggs requires rapid mineral saturation, often the result of cave collapse, volcanic ash, or elemental intrusion. Yet several specimens recovered by the Navar Dragon Guild show no evidence of such events. Instead, the stone encasing the eggs appears to have grown inward, as though drawn to the latent power within the shells.

I posit that certain eggs never hatch because they are never meant to. Dragons, ancient beyond our recorded epochs, may produce broods not for continuation, but for containment. A dragon may choose to die guarding what must never emerge.

The fragments recently acquired?matching curvature and shell density to late-era coastal dragons?display internal lattice fractures consistent with suppressed internal growth. These were viable eggs. They were halted.

Why a mother dragon would willingly die to ensure this outcome remains the unanswered question. But I caution any who would reassemble such fragments: a dragon egg is not inert simply because it is dead.

Some silences are maintained deliberately.
endmsg
