Fragments of Continuance: What Dragon Fossils Still Contain

It is a comforting fiction that fossils are dead things. Bone turned to stone. Power leached away by time. This book exists to correct that dangerous assumption.

Dragon fossils retain structure not merely physical but metaphysical. The crystalline patterns found in fossilized dragon bone mirror those of active ley conduits, though inert to casual inspection. When properly stimulated?through heat, resonance, or ritual?they respond.

Egg fragments are the most volatile of these remains. Unlike bone, which stabilizes with age, egg material retains an unfinished quality. It is potential without direction. Experiments conducted (regrettably, prior to Guild prohibition) demonstrated that powdered shell, when used in forging or enchantment, amplifies intent rather than power. The weapon does not strike harder?it strikes truer.

This may explain the persistence of dragon-tooth and shell artifacts in ancient arsenals. The material does not grant strength; it enforces purpose. Such items are resistant to enchantment drift and unusually loyal to their wielder?s initial resolve.

However, there are limits. Reconstituting egg fragments beyond their original form risks recreating aspects of the developmental lattice. One does not hatch a dragon?but one may awaken the idea of one.

I note with unease that the fragments recently delivered to the Hall of the Preserved Wing show minimal decay despite their age. They were not preserved accidentally. Something wanted them to last.

If the hero who recovered these pieces reads this book, I offer this warning: dragons do not always guard their young so they may live. Sometimes they guard them so that no one else must face what they would have become.
